WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.640
Let’s say you really need
to find reliable information

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:07.840
about the best diet for
high blood pressure,

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:10.000
or heart disease,
or diabetes.

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:11.360
Where do you go?

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:15.920
Do you go to a website
sponsored by Big Pharma

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:18.720
that wants to sell you
pills to fix your problem?

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:21.760
Or, do you want
to treat the cause?

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:25.280
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:26.800
with the latest peer-
reviewed research

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:30.320
on the best ways to eat
healthy and live longer.

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:35.360
Today we ask, why doesn’t
everyone just eat healthier?

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:37.840
Right? It seems like
such a simple solution

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:39.440
to so many of our problems.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:43.280
As it turns out, the
answer is complicated.

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:44.960
Here’s our first story.

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:48.960
Yes, media messages surrounding
nutrition are often

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:52.160
inconsistent and confusing,
but many Americans know

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:54.000
what constitutes a healthy diet.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:57.040
I mean, does anyone
really think drinking brown

00:00:57.040 --> 00:00:59.200
carbonated sugar water
is good for them?

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:02.080
At issue is that they don’t appear to be

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:04.560
translating their knowledge into action.

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:07.680
There are a number of reasons
people have such difficulty

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:09.680
changing their dietary behaviors.

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:12.320
While ignorance and
confusion may play a part,

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:15.680
the motivation to change is
likely much more important.

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:18.880
Certainly, we are living in
a world that pushes us to eat

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:21.840
whatever we want, regardless of
the long-term consequences.

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:25.520
But one of the major problems in
getting people to change their

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:29.760
behavior is the need to get them
to recognize the need to change.

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:33.040
For example, if you ask
people how much meat they eat,

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:36.880
or how much greasy food, eggs,
sweets, alcohol, butter,

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:40.640
they claim to be eating less
than the average person.

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:44.160
So, if people think they’re
at less risk than others,

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:47.520
they may dismiss advice
to eat more healthfully,

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:51.920
thinking that they already eat
healthier. Maybe they are?

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:55.920
No, people rated their own
eating behavior as healthier

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:59.520
on average even when their actual
eating habits were terrible.

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:03.520
For this reason, maybe health-
promotion campaigns need to make

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:06.960
people aware of how
badly they are eating.

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:10.960
But when you do that,
a strange thing happens.

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:14.000
If you challenge people with
the reality of what the average

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:16.960
person actually eats, they
change their answer to make

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:20.080
themselves appear as though they’re
still healthier than average.

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:23.920
When people's favorable
comparisons on risky behaviors

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:26.240
are threatened, they not
only reduce their estimates

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:28.320
of how often they engage
in those behaviors—

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:30.160
"oh, I don’t eat that much meat”—

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:34.080
but they also attenuate the
significance of those behaviors.

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:35.840
“Meat’s not that bad for you anyway.”

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:41.280
It’s the same personal fable
that smokers tell themselves.

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:43.520
Studies show that smokers
have a strong tendency

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:46.560
to underestimate all
smoking-related risks,

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:50.240
developing a series of
illusions and false beliefs

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:53.360
to support their choice
to keep on smoking.

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:57.520
Why do so many people continue
to light up in spite of

00:02:57.520 --> 00:02:59.520
smoking’s harmful
effects to their health?

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:03.840
For many of the same reasons people
continue to eat unhealthy food.

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:06.480
First, they convince
themselves that they are less

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:09.920
at risk than others who
engage in the same behavior.

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:14.640
And in addition to this optimism
bias, smokers underestimate

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:17.440
the extent to which smoking
elevates lung cancer risk,

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:21.200
thinking two-pack-a-day smokers
only have like five times the risk

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:24.720
of getting lung cancer, when their
actual risk is 20-fold higher.

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:28.080
And anyway, many
smokers believe lung cancer

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:30.560
is mainly determined by one’s genes.

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:35.920
Many food-related hazards
share this same optimistic bias,

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:38.800
such as heart attacks and
heart disease (our #1 killer),

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:41.040
obesity, diabetes, and all the rest.

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:44.400
People are often quite
ingenious in finding reasons for

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:47.280
believing that their own risk is less

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:50.160
than the risk faced by their peers.

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:53.760
So, maybe public health advocates
need to be just as ingenious

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:57.520
in understanding the origins
of this unrealistic optimism

00:03:57.520 --> 00:03:59.600
and in finding approaches
that help people gain a more

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:02.080
accurate picture of
their own vulnerability.

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:05.920
All sorts of work is being done
trying to reduce or eliminate

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:09.280
this bias, “but we must
consider the possibility that

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:13.040
reductions in optimistic
bias may lead to reductions

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:15.680
in self-esteem and
psychological well-being,”

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:18.320
if people start to realize
just how much risk they

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:22.560
truly face and how much they
have themselves to blame.

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:27.360
This reminds me of the tightrope
wire health professionals

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:30.000
have to walk, telling
people how much power

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:32.000
we all have over getting cancer.

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:36.000
This is the oft-cited paper
that calculated that we may

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:39.920
be able to prevent approximately
90 percent of human cancers.

00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:43.360
(But by “present trends,” the
researcher was talking about

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:47.040
present trends of the 1960s
when the paper was published.)

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:51.440
They remain true to this day, though,
about a half century later.

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:55.200
Genetic factors are not the
major causes of chronic diseases.

00:04:55.840 --> 00:04:58.720
Using identical twins to see
how much was really just

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:02.000
in your genes, of all the chronic
diseases they looked at,

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:05.200
cancers had the lowest
genetic component,

00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:08.560
again only about 10 percent
attributable to bad genes.

00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:12.240
What runs in families is bad habits.

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:16.240
But when you go out there and
tell everyone the good news

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:19.120
about how much power we
have over not getting cancer,

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:23.200
what about the people
who already have it?

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:25.520
When people are diagnosed
with cancer, they often ask,

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:29.280
“Why me? Did I do something
wrong? Is this my fault?”

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:34.000
So you can imagine how the
message of, “well yeah, kinda,”

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:37.920
could be destructive for
patients or survivors.

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:41.360
In other words, a message that
is intended to empower people in

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:46.080
a prevention context could just
make cancer victims feel guilty.

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:51.760
But the truth is still the truth,
no matter how difficult it may be.

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:55.200
So, what we have to do is
try to guide patients to switch

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:59.280
from feelings of guilt to a
“responsibility” approach.

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:00.640
They have personal control;

00:06:00.640 --> 00:06:03.520
they can make different
choices from then on.

00:06:03.520 --> 00:06:06.480
We need to give them a sense
of agency in their life.

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:11.520
Better, though, to try to take
those steps before you get cancer.

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:17.120
In our next story, we look at how
most deaths in the United States

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:20.400
are preventable and
related to nutrition.

00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:24.000
Does it really matter what we eat?

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:27.280
Well, the good news is,
we have tremendous power

00:06:27.840 --> 00:06:31.040
over our health destiny and longevity—
the majority of premature death

00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:35.040
and disability is preventable
with a healthy enough diet.

00:06:36.160 --> 00:06:38.560
It's. . . the. . . food.

00:06:38.560 --> 00:06:44.240
Most deaths in the United States
are preventable and related to nutrition.

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:47.360
According to the most rigorous analysis
of risk factors ever published,

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:49.040
The Global Burden of Disease Study,

00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:50.800
funded by the Bill &amp; Melinda
Gates Foundation,

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:53.760
the number one cause
of death in the United States,

00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:55.680
and the number one
cause of disability,

00:06:55.680 --> 00:07:00.640
is our diet, which has bumped
tobacco smoking to #2.

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:04.160
Smoking now only kills about a
half million Americans every year,

00:07:04.160 --> 00:07:08.400
whereas our diet kills hundreds
of thousands more.

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:14.320
What we eat is the #1
determinant of how long we live.

00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:18.720
What we eat is what determines
most whether we'll die prematurely.

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:22.960
What we eat is what determines most
whether we become disabled or not.

00:07:24.160 --> 00:07:27.280
So, if our diet is the #1 cause
of death and disability,

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:30.880
if most deaths in the United States
are preventable and related to nutrition,

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:35.920
then obviously nutrition is the #1
thing taught in medical school, right?

00:07:37.120 --> 00:07:42.240
Obviously it's the #1 thing your doctor
talks to you about at every visit, right?

00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:45.280
Unfortunately, doctors suffer from

00:07:45.280 --> 00:07:48.720
a severe nutrition deficiency —
in education.

00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:52.080
Most doctors are just never taught
about the impact healthy nutrition

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:53.440
can have on the course of illness,

00:07:53.440 --> 00:07:57.840
and so they graduate without this
powerful tool in their medical toolbox.

00:07:58.400 --> 00:08:00.640
Now, there are also
institutional barriers,

00:08:00.640 --> 00:08:02.800
such as time constraints
and lack of reimbursement.

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:06.000
In general, doctors aren't
paid for counseling people

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:08.320
on how to take better
care of themselves.

00:08:08.320 --> 00:08:10.400
Of course, the drug
companies also play a role

00:08:10.400 --> 00:08:13.120
in influencing medical
education and practice.

00:08:13.120 --> 00:08:14.560
Ask your doctor
when's the last time

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:16.720
they were taken out to
dinner by Big Broccoli;

00:08:16.720 --> 00:08:18.160
it's probably been awhile.

00:08:19.280 --> 00:08:22.480
That's why I started
NutritionFacts.org.

00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:25.840
It's the tool I wish I had
in medical training.

00:08:26.960 --> 00:08:31.040
NutritionFacts.org is a free, nonprofit,
science-based public service,

00:08:31.040 --> 00:08:35.520
providing daily updates on the latest
in nutrition research via bite-sized videos.

00:08:35.520 --> 00:08:39.840
There are videos on more than 2,000
health and nutrition topics—all free—

00:08:40.560 --> 00:08:43.200
with new videos and articles
uploaded every day

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:46.320
on the latest in
evidence-based nutrition.

00:08:47.200 --> 00:08:48.160
What a concept.

00:08:49.040 --> 00:08:51.360
Finally today,
we know for a fact

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:52.640
that a healthy plant-based diet

00:08:52.640 --> 00:08:56.000
could help reverse the
#1 killer of men and women.

00:08:56.000 --> 00:08:59.280
So shouldn’t that
be the default diet,

00:08:59.280 --> 00:09:00.400
until proven otherwise?

00:09:01.040 --> 00:09:06.240
There is only one diet that's ever
been proven to reverse heart disease

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:10.480
in the majority of patients: a diet
centered around whole plant foods.

00:09:10.480 --> 00:09:14.320
So anytime anyone tries to sell
you on some new diet,

00:09:14.320 --> 00:09:16.720
do me a favor; ask them
one simple question:

00:09:16.720 --> 00:09:20.560
"Has it been proven to
reverse heart disease?"

00:09:20.560 --> 00:09:23.680
You know, the number one reason
you and all your loved ones will die.

00:09:23.680 --> 00:09:27.120
If the answer is, "No," why
would you even consider it?

00:09:28.240 --> 00:09:30.880
Only one diet has ever
been proven to do that.

00:09:30.880 --> 00:09:34.400
That's not cherry picking;
there's only one cherry.

00:09:35.440 --> 00:09:38.240
In fact, if that's all a plant-
based diet could do—

00:09:38.880 --> 00:09:41.600
reverse the #1 killer
of men and women—

00:09:42.720 --> 00:09:46.160
shouldn't that be the default
diet until proven otherwise?

00:09:46.160 --> 00:09:49.680
And the fact that it can also be effective
in preventing, arresting, and reversing

00:09:49.680 --> 00:09:53.360
other leading killers like
type 2 diabetes, and hypertension

00:09:53.360 --> 00:09:57.760
would seem to make the case for
plant-based eating simply overwhelming.

00:09:57.760 --> 00:10:00.160
So why don't more
doctors prescribe it?

00:10:00.160 --> 00:10:03.840
How could there be such
a disconnect between the science

00:10:04.400 --> 00:10:06.480
and mainstream medical practice?

00:10:06.480 --> 00:10:09.280
Well look, it took 25 years

00:10:09.280 --> 00:10:12.800
before the first Surgeon General's
report against smoking came out.

00:10:12.800 --> 00:10:18.640
It took more than 7,000 studies, and
the deaths of countless smokers,

00:10:18.640 --> 00:10:21.840
before the powers-that-be
officially recognized the link.

00:10:21.840 --> 00:10:24.320
(You'd think that after
the first 6,000 studies

00:10:24.320 --> 00:10:26.400
they could have given people
a little heads up or something?)

00:10:27.760 --> 00:10:29.680
Powerful industry, right?

00:10:29.680 --> 00:10:32.160
In fact, even after the Surgeon
General's report came out,

00:10:32.160 --> 00:10:34.080
the medical community
still dragged their feet.

00:10:34.080 --> 00:10:36.800
The American Medical Association
actually went on record

00:10:36.800 --> 00:10:40.560
refusing to endorse the
Surgeon General's report. Why?

00:10:40.560 --> 00:10:42.960
Could it have been because
they had just been handed

00:10:42.960 --> 00:10:46.000
a 10 million dollar check from
the tobacco industry?

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:47.600
Maybe. . .

00:10:47.600 --> 00:10:50.800
So we know why the AMA may have
been sucking up to the tobacco industry,

00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:53.280
but why weren't individual
doctors speaking out?

00:10:54.080 --> 00:10:57.680
There were a few gallant souls ahead
of their time writing in, as there are today,

00:10:57.680 --> 00:11:01.040
standing up against industries
killing millions, but why not more?

00:11:01.680 --> 00:11:07.040
Maybe it's because the majority of
physicians themselves smoked cigarettes,

00:11:07.040 --> 00:11:09.920
just like the majority of physicians
today continue to eat foods

00:11:09.920 --> 00:11:13.920
that are contributing to our
epidemics of dietary disease.

00:11:13.920 --> 00:11:16.720
What was the AMA's
rallying cry back then?

00:11:16.720 --> 00:11:18.960
"Everything in moderation.

00:11:18.960 --> 00:11:21.920
"Extensive scientific studies have
proven that smoking in moderation...

00:11:21.920 --> 00:11:25.200
"Oh, that's fine."
Sound familiar?

00:11:25.760 --> 00:11:28.160
Consumption of animal foods
and processed foods may cause

00:11:28.160 --> 00:11:31.520
at least 14 million deaths
around the world every year.

00:11:31.520 --> 00:11:34.560
14 million people dead.

00:11:35.280 --> 00:11:39.680
Those of us involved in this evidence-
based nutrition revolution are part

00:11:39.680 --> 00:11:44.560
of a movement with 14 million
lives in the balance every year.

00:11:46.160 --> 00:11:47.600
Plant-based diets may
now be considered

00:11:47.600 --> 00:11:50.400
the nutritional equivalent
of quitting smoking.

00:11:50.400 --> 00:11:52.880
How many more people have
to die though before the CDC

00:11:52.880 --> 00:11:57.680
encourages people not to wait for open-heart
surgery to start eating healthy as well?

00:11:59.440 --> 00:12:02.400
Until the system changes, we have

00:12:02.400 --> 00:12:05.680
to take personal responsibility for our
own health, for our family's health.

00:12:05.680 --> 00:12:10.000
We can't wait until society catches
up to the science again,

00:12:10.000 --> 00:12:12.640
because it's a matter
of life and death.

00:12:14.560 --> 00:12:18.960
Last year, Dr. Kim Williams became President
of the American College of Cardiology.

00:12:18.960 --> 00:12:20.160
He was asked in an interview

00:12:20.160 --> 00:12:23.520
why he follows his own advice
to eat a plant-based diet?

00:12:23.520 --> 00:12:31.130
"I don't mind dying," Dr. Williams replied,
"I just don't want it to be my own fault."

00:12:31.920 --> 00:12:34.880
We would love it if you could
share with us your stories

00:12:34.880 --> 00:12:38.160
about reinventing your health
through evidence-based nutrition.

00:12:38.160 --> 00:12:40.720
Go to nutritionfacts.org/testimonials.

00:12:40.720 --> 00:12:44.480
We may share it on our social
media to help inspire others.

00:12:44.480 --> 00:12:48.160
To see any graphs, charts, graphics,
images, or studies mentioned here,

00:12:48.160 --> 00:12:51.440
please go to the Nutrition Facts
Podcast landing page.

00:12:51.440 --> 00:12:54.000
There you’ll find all the
detailed information you need –

00:12:54.000 --> 00:12:58.160
plus, links to all of the sources
we cite to each of these topics.

